    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 221850\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1150 AM PDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Additional rounds of rain and storms expected across most of the \n  area today.\n  \n* Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures expected this weekend\n  and early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Wednesday.\n\nLatest satellite and radar imagery shows two distinct precipitation \nregimes within the forecast area. The most robust convection exists \nin Lincoln County where meso-analysis shows MUCAPE ~1000 J/kg and \neffective shear of 40-50 knots. This environment will keep a \nmarginal threat of severe weather (mainly hail) and flash flooding \npresent through most of the morning hours. The second regime is more \nassociated with vorticity advection and cold air aloft with the low. \nThis activity is much weaker, but persistent moderate rain with a \nfew storms mixed in may cause minor flooding in the most susceptible \nareas of Death Valley this morning. As the low pushes inland today, \nshowers and storms will spread east. The greatest coverage is \nexpected along and north of I-15 and AZ Strip (PoPs 50-90%), with \nmore isolated activity between I-15 and I-40 (PoPs 20-50%). The \nlower instability today should limit the severe potential, but cold \nair aloft and sufficient vertical shear will keep a marginal risk of \nsevere hail in our northeastern areas. By midnight, precipitation is \nforecast to be largely out of our CWA, allowing dry conditions to \nprevail overnight. Isolated shower chances (10-25%) return Friday \nafternoon as the trough lingers overhead, but most locations remain \ndry.\n\nEnsemble guidance has the trough pinching off into a closed low over \nthe weekend, which means it will be slow to exit the region. On \nSaturday, models are in good agreement that the low will be centered \nover northwestern or north-central AZ. This keeps low shower chances \n(10-25%) over eastern Mohave County, while dry conditions and gusty \nnorth winds occur to the west. Dry and calm weather finally returns \nto the entire forecast area on Sunday and sticks around through \nWednesday. During this time, temperatures are forecast to be near or \nslightly below normal for late October.\n\n&& \n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast Package... \nScattered showers in the vicinity of the field will persist into \nthis evening before ending by 04Z. Initially, most of the activity \nis expected to be over the higher terrain west of the field, but \nguidance is showing a window between 22Z and 02Z when showers will \nhave a better chance of directly impacting the field. Away from \nshowers, winds should generally be southerly with gusts to 20 knots. \nHowever, winds may be affected by shower activity, resulting in \nerratic wind directions at times. An isolated thunderstorm is also \nnot out of the question later this afternoon. Winds will diminish \novernight and Friday, with speeds staying below 8 knots. CIGs should \ngenerally remain above 8kft AGL but could fall to 5kft to 6kft AGL \nin any showers. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Scattered showers and \nisolated thunderstorms will continue to be possible across a large \nportion of the region this afternoon and evening, with the heaviest \nactivity located over the eastern third. CIGs could fall as low as \n5kft to 6kft AGL in and near the stronger activity, while away from \nprecipitation, cloud bases should generally remain AOA 8kft AGL. \nLocally erratic winds are also likely near showers and storms. \nOtherwise, gusty south to west winds are expected at most regional \nterminals into this evening. Lighter winds will be seen overnight, \nwith speeds falling below 10 knots in most areas.   \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Planz\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
